L["delilah"] = """Samson was twenty years into his time as a judge of Israel, and the Philistines still ruled the land. He had killed many of them in revenge, but he had not freed his people. His weakness was women. He fell in love with a woman named Delilah, who lived in the Valley of Sorek. The Philistine rulers went to her and said, see if you can lure him into showing you the secret of his great strength, and how we can overpower him. Each of us will give you eleven hundred shekels of silver. That was a huge amount of money.

So Delilah asked Samson, tell me the secret of your great strength. He lied. If anyone ties me with seven fresh bowstrings that have not been dried, I'll become as weak as any other man. She tied him with seven bowstrings while men were hiding in the room. She cried out, Samson, the Philistines are upon you! He snapped the bowstrings like a thread of tow that comes close to a flame.

She said, you have made a fool of me. You lied to me. Please tell me how you can be tied. He said, new ropes that have never been used. She tried it. He snapped them off his arms like thread. A third time he told her it was the seven braids of his hair woven into a loom. She wove them in. He pulled up the loom and the pin.

Each time, Delilah would cry out that the Philistines were on him, and each time they were. Samson never learned. The Bible says that with such nagging she prodded him day after day until he was sick to death of it. So he told her everything. No razor has ever been used on my head, because I have been a Nazirite dedicated to God since birth. If my head were shaved, my strength would leave me, and I would become as weak as any other man.

Delilah saw that he had told her the truth. She sent word to the rulers. This time, come back once more. He has told me everything. The rulers came with the silver in their hands. She let him go to sleep on her lap, and called a man to shave off the seven braids of his hair. Then she began to subdue him, and his strength left him. She called, Samson, the Philistines are upon you! He awoke from his sleep and thought, I'll go out as before and shake myself free. But he did not know that the Lord had left him.

Then comes one of the saddest lines in the Bible. Then the Philistines seized him, gouged out his eyes and took him down to Gaza. Binding him with bronze shackles, they set him to grinding grain in the prison. He was a slave, and blind, and working a mill like an ox. But the hair on his head began to grow again after it had been shaved.

Why does this matter? Samson's strength was never really in his hair. It came from the Lord. The hair was a sign of his promise to God, and when he let the promise be broken bit by bit, the Lord's Spirit left him. The tragedy is that he didn't even notice. He woke up and thought he would shake himself free as before.

It is a warning for anyone with a gift. We can lose it so slowly that we don't notice until it is gone. Samson played with temptation again and again. He lied about the secret three times, which meant he was keeping it close to the edge. It was only a matter of time until he gave in.

What happened after? Samson was alone, blind, and in the dark. His hair grew back. And that is where the Bible turns the story, as we will see in the next scene. Even the lowest point of his life was not the end.

What does this mean for us today? Be careful what you play with. Samson thought he could flirt with disaster and always get away. We do that too, with habits, relationships, and decisions that seem small. Each time, we think we can walk back from the edge.

It also means that the people we trust the most can hurt us, and that we can hurt the people who love us. Delilah was paid to betray him, but the real problem was that Samson let her get close to the one secret that mattered.

That is why this story is so important. It is a warning about what happens to people who are given much and take it lightly. And yet it is also the first half of a story of grace. The man who lost everything in this chapter would turn to God in the next one."""

L["samson-death"] = """Samson was in prison in Gaza. He was blind, his head was shaved, and he was chained to a grinding wheel. The Philistines thought they had won. The Bible says that Samson had been a judge of Israel for twenty years, and he was now a slave. But the hair on his head began to grow again.

The Philistine rulers gathered to offer a great sacrifice to their god Dagon, and to celebrate. They said, our god has delivered Samson, our enemy, into our hands. When the people saw him, they praised their god, saying, our god has delivered our enemy into our hands, the one who laid waste our land and multiplied our slain. While they were in high spirits, they shouted, bring out Samson to entertain us.

So they called Samson out of the prison, and he performed for them. They stood him between the pillars. The temple was crowded with men and women. All the rulers of the Philistines were there, and on the roof were about three thousand men and women watching Samson perform. It was a large building, a place built to hold the whole Philistine leadership and a great crowd.

Samson said to the servant who held his hand, put me where I can feel the pillars that support the temple, so that I may lean against them. Then Samson prayed to the Lord. Sovereign Lord, remember me. Please, God, strengthen me just once more, and let me with one blow get revenge on the Philistines for my two eyes.

Look at that prayer. For years Samson had acted as if his strength belonged to him. Now he prayed, God, please. It was the first time in his story that he asked God for something instead of taking it. He was humble. He was blind, and he had nothing left, and he knew it. He remembered that God was the source.

Then Samson reached toward the two central pillars on which the temple stood. Bracing himself against them, his right hand on the one and his left hand on the other, Samson said, let me die with the Philistines! Then he pushed with all his strength, and the temple fell on the rulers and all the people in it. Those he killed at his death were more than those he had killed during his life.

His brothers and his father's whole family went down to get his body. They brought him back and buried him between Zorah and Eshtaol, in the tomb of his father Manoah. He had led Israel for twenty years.

Why does this matter? Samson's last act is a picture of a man whose strength came back when he turned back to God. God answered the prayer of a man who had thrown away almost everything. That is grace. It doesn't mean that the damage was undone. Samson lost his eyes, his freedom, and his life. But it does mean that God was still working, and he was not finished with Samson.

The book of Hebrews lists Samson among the heroes of faith. That is a surprise, and an encouragement. He is not on the list for his record. He is there because at the end, he trusted God.

What happened after? Samson began to deliver Israel from the Philistines, as the angel had said, but it was not completed. The book of Judges ends in a dark mess, with the repeated line, in those days Israel had no king, and everyone did as they saw fit. The people needed a better king. Samuel and David would come, and ultimately Jesus.

Jesus has a similarity to Samson, but he is the better version. Samson died with his arms stretched out between two pillars, and in his death he defeated more enemies than he did in life. Jesus died with his arms stretched out, and in his death he broke the power of sin and death.

What does this mean for us today? It is never too late to turn back to God. Samson was blind, chained, and humiliated. He had no one left. But even there, the Lord heard his prayer.

It also means that our failures do not cancel God's purposes. Samson's life was messy, and the cost was high. But God still used his last act to do what he'd said he would do. If you feel that you have wasted your gifts, this story says that God can still hear you.

That is why this story is so important. It shows the one who is strong enough to save and gracious enough to hear the prayer of a failure. Samson's last prayer was short, and it began with the right word. Lord."""

L["ruth-naomi"] = """This story happened in the days when the judges ruled Israel, a time of violence and confusion. But the book of Ruth is a quiet one. It is about a family, a famine, and two women. A man named Elimelech from Bethlehem, which means house of bread, took his wife Naomi and two sons to live in the country of Moab, because there was no food in Judah. Moab was a neighbor that Israel had a hard history with.

Elimelech died. The sons married Moabite women, one named Orpah and the other Ruth. After about ten years, both sons died too. Naomi was left alone in a foreign country, with no husband and no sons. In that world, a widow without a family had no support at all.

Naomi heard that the Lord had come to the aid of his people by providing food for them back in Bethlehem. She decided to go home. Her two daughters-in-law started with her. But on the road, Naomi told them to go back. May the Lord show you kindness, as you have shown to your dead and to me. May the Lord grant that each of you will find rest in the home of another husband. Then she kissed them, and they wept aloud.

They said, we will go back with you to your people. But Naomi insisted. Why would you come with me? Am I going to have any more sons, who could become your husbands? No, my daughters. It is more bitter for me than for you, because the Lord's hand has turned against me. They wept again. Orpah kissed her mother-in-law goodbye and went back. This wasn't wrong. It was a reasonable decision. But Ruth clung to Naomi.

Naomi said, look, your sister-in-law is going back to her people and her gods. Go back with her. Ruth answered with some of the most beautiful words in the Bible. Don't urge me to leave you or to turn back from you. Where you go I will go, and where you stay I will stay. Your people will be my people and your God my God. Where you die I will die, and there I will be buried. May the Lord deal with me, be it ever so severely, if even death separates you and me.

Ruth was not an Israelite. She was a foreigner, a woman, and a widow. Yet she gave up her home, her family, and her own gods to follow Naomi and Naomi's God. She had nothing to gain and everything to lose. When Naomi saw that she was determined, she stopped urging her.

They went on until they came to Bethlehem. The whole town was stirred, and the women asked, can this be Naomi? She said, don't call me Naomi, which means pleasant. Call me Mara, which means bitter, because the Almighty has made my life very bitter. I went away full, but the Lord has brought me back empty. They arrived in Bethlehem at the beginning of the barley harvest.

Why does this matter? Ruth's loyalty is a picture of what the Bible calls hesed, the Hebrew word for faithful, covenant love that doesn't quit. She chose to stay with someone who could give her nothing. It is one of the clearest pictures of love in the Old Testament.

It is also the story of a woman from Moab, an outsider, who came to trust the God of Israel. The Bible shows again that God's plan was always bigger than one nation. Ruth is one of only two books in the Bible named for a woman, and she was a foreigner.

What happened after? Ruth went out to glean in the fields, and she met a man named Boaz. That is the next scene. In the end, she married him and had a son, Obed. Obed became the grandfather of King David, and through David's line came Jesus. Ruth is in the family tree of the Messiah.

What does this mean for us today? Naomi is a picture of anyone who feels empty. She was honest about her pain, and she was heard. She said I am bitter, and the story doesn't say she was wrong. God didn't leave her there.

Ruth is a picture of faithfulness. She stayed when it would have been easier to leave. She did it for someone who couldn't pay her back. Real love is often just that, staying.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that God is working in the background of everyday sorrow. The famine, the funerals, and the road home were all part of a plan that would reach to Bethlehem and a manger. The house of bread was where the Bread of Life would one day be born."""

L["ruth-boaz"] = """Ruth and Naomi had arrived in Bethlehem at the start of the barley harvest. They were poor. They had no land and no income. But the law God gave Israel had a provision for people like them. When farmers harvested their fields, they were not to go over the field a second time or pick up what was dropped. That was left for the poor, the foreigner, the widow, and the orphan. It was called gleaning.

Ruth said to Naomi, let me go to the fields and pick up the leftover grain behind anyone in whose eyes I find favor. Naomi said, go ahead, my daughter. As it turned out, she found herself working in a field belonging to Boaz, who was from the clan of Elimelech. The Bible says, as it turned out. It doesn't say that she planned it. This is how the writer shows God working behind the scenes.

Just then Boaz arrived from Bethlehem and greeted the harvesters. The Lord be with you! The Lord bless you! he said. He asked his foreman, whose young woman is that? The foreman said, she is the Moabite who came back with Naomi. She said, please let me glean and gather among the sheaves behind the harvesters. She came into the field and has kept working steadily from morning until now, except for a short rest in the shelter.

Boaz spoke to Ruth. My daughter, listen to me. Don't go and glean in another field and don't go away from here. Stay here with the women who work for me. I have told the men not to lay a hand on you. And whenever you are thirsty, go and get a drink from the water jars the men have filled. This was a real protection. A foreign woman alone in the fields was vulnerable.

Ruth bowed down with her face to the ground. Why have I found such favor in your eyes that you notice me, a foreigner? Boaz replied, I've been told all about what you have done for your mother-in-law since the death of your husband, how you left your father and mother and your homeland and came to live with a people you did not know before. May the Lord repay you for what you have done. May you be richly rewarded by the Lord, the God of Israel, under whose wings you have come to take refuge.

At mealtime, Boaz invited her to come eat with them. He offered her bread and roasted grain, and she ate until she was full and had some left over. When she got up to glean again, Boaz ordered his men to let her gather even among the sheaves, and to pull out some stalks for her from the bundles and leave them for her to pick up. She gleaned until evening. She threshed the barley, and it was about an ephah, which is around thirty pounds.

She carried it back to town and showed her mother-in-law. Naomi asked, where did you glean today? Where did you work? Blessed be the man who took notice of you! When Ruth told her it was Boaz, Naomi said, the Lord bless him! He has not stopped showing his kindness to the living and the dead. That man is our close relative. He is one of our guardian-redeemers. A guardian-redeemer was a relative who had the right and the duty to rescue a family member in need, to buy back the land and to marry the widow.

For the rest of the barley and wheat harvest, Ruth stayed close to the women who worked for Boaz and kept gleaning. In the next chapter, Ruth went to Boaz on the threshing floor and asked him to be her redeemer. He agreed. He went to the town gate and arranged for the closer relative to give up his claim. Then he bought Elimelech's land and married Ruth.

Why does this matter? Boaz is a picture of Christ. He is the redeemer, the one who sees a stranger in need, pays the price to rescue her, and brings her into his family. Ruth could not save herself, and she had no claim on him. Everything was by grace. The kindness began with him.

What happened after? Ruth and Boaz had a son named Obed, and the women of Bethlehem said to Naomi, praise be to the Lord, who this day has not left you without a guardian-redeemer. Naomi took the child in her arms, and he was like a son to her. Obed was the father of Jesse, and Jesse was the father of David. A story that started with death ended with a birth.

What does this mean for us today? It says that God is at work in ordinary things. A woman went out to pick up leftover grain. It doesn't look like history. But it was exactly where God's plan was moving.

It also says something about how we treat the poor. Boaz made it easy for Ruth. He left extra grain for her and protected her dignity. That is how God's people are meant to treat anyone in need, with generosity, not just minimum compliance.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that the Redeemer was already at work in the Old Testament, in a small field, with a foreign widow, and a kind man. And it assures us that a God who cares about the gleaners cares about us."""

L["goliath"] = """For forty days, the Philistine army and the army of Israel faced each other across the Valley of Elah. The Philistines were on one hill and the Israelites on another. And every morning and evening a giant named Goliath stepped out and challenged Israel. He was from Gath, and he stood over nine feet tall. He wore bronze armor that weighed about 125 pounds, and carried a spear with an iron tip weighing fifteen pounds. His shield bearer walked ahead of him.

Goliath shouted, why do you come out and line up for battle? Am I not a Philistine, and are you not the servants of Saul? Choose a man and have him come down to me. If he is able to fight and kill me, we will become your subjects. But if I overcome him and kill him, you will become our subjects. This day I defy the armies of Israel! Give me a man and let us fight each other. When Saul and the Israelites heard this, they were dismayed and terrified.

King Saul was the tallest man in Israel. If anyone should have gone out, it was him. But he was afraid too. For forty days, no one in the army of Israel did anything.

Meanwhile, back in Bethlehem, a young shepherd named David was caring for his father's sheep. His three oldest brothers were in Saul's army. One day his father Jesse sent him to the camp with bread and cheese for his brothers. David arrived just as the army was going out to the battle line, shouting the war cry. He heard Goliath's challenge, and he saw the men of Israel run in fear.

David asked, what will be done for the man who kills this Philistine and removes this disgrace from Israel? Who is this uncircumcised Philistine that he should defy the armies of the living God? His oldest brother, Eliab, was angry. Why have you come down here? And with whom did you leave those few sheep? I know how conceited you are and how wicked your heart is. David said, now what have I done? Can't I even speak?

Word got to Saul, and he sent for David. David told him, let no one lose heart on account of this Philistine. Your servant will go and fight him. Saul said, you are only a young man, and he has been a warrior from his youth. David said, your servant has been keeping his father's sheep. When a lion or a bear came and carried off a sheep from the flock, I went after it, struck it and rescued the sheep from its mouth. The Lord who rescued me from the paw of the lion and the paw of the bear will rescue me from the hand of this Philistine.

Saul put his own armor on David, but David couldn't walk in it. He took it off. Instead he took his staff in his hand, chose five smooth stones from the stream, and put them in his shepherd's bag. With his sling in his hand, he approached Goliath.

Goliath looked David over and saw that he was little more than a boy, and he despised him. Am I a dog, that you come at me with sticks? David said, you come against me with sword and spear and javelin, but I come against you in the name of the Lord Almighty, the God of the armies of Israel, whom you have defied. This day the Lord will deliver you into my hands. The whole world will know that there is a God in Israel. All those gathered here will know that it is not by sword or spear that the Lord saves. For the battle is the Lord's.

David ran quickly toward the battle line to meet him. He reached into his bag, took out a stone, and slung it. It struck the Philistine on the forehead and sank in, and he fell facedown on the ground. David had no sword in his hand. He ran and stood over him, took Goliath's own sword, and cut off his head. When the Philistines saw that their hero was dead, they turned and ran, and Israel chased them.

Why does this matter? David wasn't chosen because he was strong. God had already chosen him through the prophet Samuel, passing over his older and taller brothers. God doesn't look at the outward appearance, but at the heart. David trusted God. And he refused to see the situation the way everyone else did. They saw a giant too big to fight. David saw a giant too big to miss.

What happened after? David became a hero, and Saul grew jealous. This began years of running from Saul. But David was also anointed to be the next king, and eventually he reigned for forty years. Through his family, Jesus would come.

What does this mean for us today? Everybody faces a giant. It might be a diagnosis, a debt, or a fear that has loomed over you for months. This story says that the size of the problem is not the main thing. Whom you trust is. David had a sling, but he had learned in private, with the lion and the bear, that God was faithful. The little victories prepared him for the big one.

It also tells us not to use someone else's armor. Saul's armor was fine, but it was not David's. God uses us as we are, with the tools we know, and we don't need to be someone else.

That is why this story is so important. It says that the battle belongs to the Lord. It teaches courage that does not come from being strong, but from knowing who is on our side. It points to Jesus, a shepherd from Bethlehem who went out alone to face the enemy, and won the victory for everyone else."""
